NNAAMMEE
     ttmmppffss — in-memory file system

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     To compile this driver into the kernel, place the following line in your
     kernel configuration file:

           ooppttiioonnss TTMMPPFFSS

     Alternatively, to load the driver as a module at boot time, place the
     following line in loader.conf(5):

           tmpfs_load="YES"

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ttmmppffss driver implements an in-memory, or ttmmppffss file system.  The
     filesystem stores both file metadata and data in main memory.  This al‐
     lows very fast and low latency accesses to the data.  The data is
     volatile.  An umount or system reboot invalidates it.  These properties
     make the filesystem's mounts suitable for fast scratch storage, like
     _/_t_m_p.

     If the system becomes low on memory and swap is configured (see
     swapon(8)), the system can transfer file data to swap space, freeing mem‐
     ory for other needs.  Metadata, including the directory content, is never
     swapped out by the current implementation.  Keep this in mind when plan‐
     ning the mount limits, especially when expecting to place many small
     files on a tmpfs mount.

     When mmap(2) is used on a file from a tmpfs mount, the swap VM object
     managing the file pages is used to implement mapping and avoid double-
     copying of the file data.  This quirk causes process inspection tools,
     like procstat(1), to report anonymous memory mappings instead of file
     mappings.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
     The following options are available when mounting ttmmppffss file systems:

     eeaassiizzee       Set the maximum memory size used by extended attributes in
                  bytes.  The default is 16 megabytes.

     eexxppoorrtt       Accept the eexxppoorrtt option for compatibility with nfsv4(4).
                  This option does nothing.

     ggiidd          Set the group ID of the root inode of the file system.  The
                  default is the mount point's GID.

     iinnooddeess       Set the maximum number of nodes available to the file sys‐
                  tem.  If not specified, the file system chooses a reasonable
                  maximum based on the file system size, which can be limited
                  with the ssiizzee option.

     mmaaxxffiilleessiizzee  Set the maximum file size in bytes.  The default is the max‐
                  imum possible value.

     mmooddee         Set the mode (in octal notation) of the root inode of the
                  file system.  The default is the mount point's mode.

     nnoommttiimmee      Disable the tracking of mtime updates caused by writes to
                  the shared mapped areas backed by ttmmppffss files.  This option
                  removes periodic scans, which downgrade read-write-mapped
                  pages to read-only to note the writes.

     nnoonncc         Do not use namecache to resolve names to files for the cre‐
                  ated mount.  This saves memory, but currently might impair
                  scalability for highly used mounts on large machines.

     nnoossyymmffoollllooww  Do not follow symlink(7)'s on the mounted file system.

     ppggrreeaadd       Enable pgcache read for the mount.

     ssiizzee         Set the total file system size in bytes, unless suffixed
                  with one of k, m, g, t, or p, which denote byte, kilobyte,
                  megabyte, gigabyte, terabyte and petabyte respectively.  If
                  zero (the default) or a value larger than SIZE_MAX -
                  PAGE_SIZE is given, the available amount of memory (includ‐
                  ing main memory and swap space) will be used.

     uuiidd          Set the user ID of the root inode of the file system.  The
                  default is the mount point's UID.

     uunniioonn        Refer to mount(8).

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     Mount a ttmmppffss memory file system:

           mount -t tmpfs tmpfs /tmp

     Configure a ttmmppffss mount via fstab(5):

           tmpfs /tmp tmpfs rw 0 0

